<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="heptadecane biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; All cyanobacterial membranes contain alkanes and/or alkenes of 1519 carbons at concentrations similar to that of chlorophyll. The most common alkanes observed are |FRAME: CPD-7946| and |FRAME: HEPTADECANE-CPD| |CITS:[17731762][McInnes80][12495426]|. These alkanes are prduced from fatty acids in three steps - activation of the fatty acid by an |FRAME: ACP &quot;acyl-carrier-protein&quot;|, reduction of the activated fatty acid to an aldehyde, and decarbonylation of the aldehyde, resulting in an alkane that is one carbon short of the original fatty acids. Since most fatty acids have an even number of carbons (fatty acids are synthesized by the addition of two carbon units), the resulting alkanes have an odd number of carbons. It should be noted that alkanes are produced in other organisms as well - for example, in the |FRAME: PWY-282 &quot;production of plant cuticular waxes&quot;| |CITS: [18251711]|, as |FRAME: PWY-7035 &quot;insect pheromones&quot;| |CITS: [7937826][10406089]|, in green algae |CITS: [1898004][15630516]|, in fungi |CITS: [18957585]| and in bacteria other than cyanobacteria |CITS:[ 6025301][15170379]|. The final step in the synthesis of n-hydrocarbons in animals and higher plants is thought to involve enzymatic decarbonylation of aldehydes to the corresponding alkanes |CITS: [3343228][1608940][12467640]|. The genes that are responsible for alkane biosynthesis in cyanobacteria, which also involve decarbonylation, have been identified and cloned from several strains of cyanobacteria |CITS: [20061450][20671186]|. While long-chain activated fatty acids can be generated in cyanobacteria &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; (via the elongase complex),it has been shown that they can also be generated from free fatty acids (provided either externally or by lipid degradation) by the action of |FRAME: MONOMER-17302| |CITS: [20061450]|. The conversion of the activated fatty acids to alkanes is catalyzed by two enzymes, the products of the |FRAME: SYNPCC7942_1594| and |FRAME: SYNPCC7942_1593| genes, which encode an |FRAME: SYNPCC7942_1594-MONOMER| and an |FRAME: SYNPCC7942_1593-MONOMER|, respectively. These two genes are located next to each other on the chromosomes of most strains that were studied, and appear to form an operon. In |FRAME: TAX-1140| the two genes reside in an operon with a third gene, &lt;i&gt;accA&lt;/i&gt;, which encodes the &amp;alpha; subunit of the carboxyl transferase component of acetyl-CoA carboxylase. Extracts of |FRAME:TAX-562| expressing the two genes contained substantial quantities of odd-chain alkanes and alkenes. |FRAME: SYNPCC7942_1593-MONOMER &quot;Aldehyde decarbonylase&quot;| was originally thought to produce |FRAME: CARBON-MONOXIDE| like the enzymes from higher plants and green algae. However, the most recent studies showed that the actual product of the cyanobacterial enzyme is |FRAME: FORMATE| |CITS: [21341652]|. There was some controversy regarding the actual reaction. While some researchers found that the enzyme requires oxygen |CITS: [21671322][21462983]|, others reported that the enzyme is much more active anaerobically |CITS: [22074177]|. However, additional work verified that the enzyme is indeed an oxygenase |CITS: [22947199]|. A mutant of |FRAME: TAX-1148| that does not produce alkanes exhibited enhanced cyclic electron flow, in which electrons from photosystem I (PSI) were returned to the |FRAME: Plastoquinols plastoquinone| pool, resulting in a slower growth rate |CITS: [26459862]|.</Description>
